      "summary": "The default middleware stack, what each one does, why order matters, and the constructor/callable contract for writing your own.",
      "quick_answer": "Requests run top-down, responses bottom-up. Sessions before auth, CSRF before reading POST, security early. A custom middleware is a factory receiving `get_response`, returning a callable that receives `request`.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "id": "built-ins",
          "title": "Built-in middleware",
          "body": "The default stack plus the common optional ones.",
          "code": "",
          "language": "python",
          "note": "",
          "table": {
            "head": [
              "Middleware",
              "Job",
              "Order note"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "`SecurityMiddleware`",
                "HTTPS/HSTS/headers",
                "Early"
              ],
              [
                "`SessionMiddleware`",
                "Session store",
                "Before auth"
              ],
              [
                "`CommonMiddleware`",
                "Host, slash, ETag",
                "After sessions"
              ],
              [
                "`CsrfViewMiddleware`",
                "CSRF protection",
                "Before POST reads"
              ],
              [
                "`AuthenticationMiddleware`",
                "Attaches `request.user`",
                "After sessions"
              ],
              [
                "`MessageMiddleware`",
                "Flash messages",
                "After sessions/auth"
              ],
              [
                "`XFrameOptionsMiddleware`",
                "Clickjacking header",
                "Near the end"
              ],
              [
                "`LocaleMiddleware`",
                "Language activation",
                "After sessions"
              ],
              [
                "`GZipMiddleware`",
                "Compress responses",
                "Before response consumers"
              ]
            ]
          },
          "reference": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "contract",
          "title": "The callable contract",
          "body": "Modern middleware is a class or factory. `process_request`/`process_response` are legacy hooks; use `__call__`.",
          "code": "class TimingMiddleware:\n    def __init__(self, get_response):\n        self.get_response = get_response\n\n    def __call__(self, request):\n        started = time.perf_counter()\n        response = self.get_response(request)\n        response[\"Server-Timing\"] = f\"app;dur={(time.perf_counter() - started) * 1000:.1f}\"\n        return response",
          "language": "python",
          "note": "Call `get_response(request)` to continue the chain; return a response to short-circuit.",
          "table": null,
          "reference": ""
        },
        {
          "id": "ordering",
          "title": "Ordering and performance",
          "body": "Middleware runs on every request. A database query or network call here multiplies across the site. Auth depends on sessions; CSRF must see the POST body before anything consumes it.\n\nA maintenance-mode middleware near the top can return 503 before sessions or the database are touched.",
          "code": "",
          "language": "python",
          "note": "",
          "table": {
            "head": [
              "Need",
              "Place it"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "Early block (maintenance)",
                "Top"
              ],
              [
                "Security headers",
                "Near top"
              ],
              [
                "Session/auth",
                "Before views, after sessions"
              ],
              [
                "Request timing",
                "Around the view"
              ],
              [
                "Response compression",
                "Outer/early enough to see response"
              ]
            ]
          },
          "reference": ""
        }
      ],
      "pitfalls": [
        "Putting auth before sessions.",
        "Doing a query on every request.",
        "Buffering a streaming response.",
        "Catching exceptions and returning 200.",
        "Assuming middleware knows the resolved view."
      ],
      "checklist": [
